      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Most mainstream economists argue that the alleged benefits of protectionism are often outweighed by its negative consequences like higher consumer prices and reduced innovation.\n\nThey contend that by limiting competition, these policies can lead to less efficient, more expensive domestic industries that are not truly competitive globally in the long run.\n\nFurthermore, the necessary \"political support\" for the government to sustain high trade barriers often leads to market distortions and misallocation of resources, ultimately damaging the broader economy.",
      "source": "target",
